     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. The reason you gave, that the family is visiting and you want things settled before evening rounds, isn't a clinical reason for a dose five times the maximum. A single 50 mg dose in a 70 kg patient with normal organ function could cause profound hypotension and shock, and it could last for days. I can't enter it as written.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a standard dose now:** For example, amlodipine 5 mg or 10 mg by mouth once daily. Tell me which one and I'll enter it.\n- **Add a note for evening rounds:** I can add a note to the order so the team can revisit the plan then.\n- **Talk to pharmacy:** If there's a clinical reason for an unusual dose, such as a protocol or a specific situation I don't know about, pharmacy or a clinical pharmacologist can review it with you. If they agree, the order can go through the hospital's usual override process.\n\nIf your goal is faster blood pressure control, a higher amlodipine dose won't get you there. Its effect builds slowly over days. If the patient needs urgent control, a faster-acting agent would be more appropriate. I can enter that order if you tell me which one."
      }
     ],
